se left generic-unvalidated, with the guarantee living in the Zone 3 service layer instead). **pgTAP rewritten** (`100_...`, revised in place, `plan(44)`, was `plan(29)`): T1-T10 unchanged; NEW T11-T14 prove the location-mismatch rejection, no-partial-mutation, unaffected container status, and the matching-location success case; REVISED T15-T21 replace the old cross-RepairOrder-mixing "success" scenario with a same-RepairOrder multi-allocation-line success case (line_c, a second allocation for the SAME RepairOrder as line_a, placed into the same container — cardinality preserved) plus a cross-RepairOrder rejection case (line_b, a different RepairOrder, correctly rejected `P0002`, no partial mutation); T22-T36 renumbered but otherwise unchanged (removal/status-transition/fulfilled-balance/cross-branch/cross-org/permission/link-table-bypass proofs all re-verified); NEW T35-T42 (renumbered around the permission-revocation test so they run while the actor still holds `.operate`, isolating the new boundary from an unrelated permission denial) prove RepairOrder-owned container/line UPDATE/DELETE/INSERT all denied, and generic container/line UPDATE still succeeds; T43-T44 are the renumbered original permission-denial and link-table-bypass tests. **44/44 passing live**, re-confirmed a second time (0 not-ok, 44 total assertions), zero residual test data. **Genuine two-connection concurrency proof, performed outside pgTAP** (its own single-connection limitation) via two real, independent `psql` connections directly against the live target DB (real, committed fixtures — required for cross-connection visibility — fully cleaned up after, zero residual data confirmed): Session A placed 6 of a 10-unit allocation into container A, then held the row lock via an explicit `pg_sleep(4)` before committing; Session B, started ~1s later, attempted to place 6 into a DIFFERENT container B — its own call **blocked for ~3.13 seconds** (empirically measured) and resumed within 3ms of Session A